= Centella Asiatica (Maṇḍūkaparṇī / Gotu Kola)

_Ayurvedic status: classical — maṇḍūkaparṇī is one of the principal medhya rasāyana (intellect-rejuvenative) drugs of the Caraka tradition, alongside brāhmī, śaṅkhapuṣpī, jyotiṣmatī, and others. The classical name-identification is_ debated _between maṇḍūkaparṇī (the modern consensus identification for Centella asiatica) and brāhmī (which is shared between Centella asiatica in some regional traditions and Bacopa monnieri in others; the_ brāhmī-naming ambiguity _is one of the standing issues in Indian dravyaguna). Chinese: classical — 積雪草 (jīxuěcǎo, "snow-piling herb") is in the Bencao tradition. Madagascan / Indian Ocean: foundational folk-medical herb. Sinhala / Sri Lankan: "gotu kola" — the English-borrowed name. Greco-Roman: not classical._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Centella asiatica_ (L.) Urban, family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae); the drug is the *whole plant* (leaves, stems, roots — the small creeping herb). Other species in the genus exist; _C. asiatica_ is the principal medicinal. _Centella_ from a Renaissance Latin coinage; _asiatica_ = "Asian."

*English:* _Centella_ (the genus name retained); *_gotu kola_* — direct transliteration of Sinhala _goṭu kola_ (ගොටු කොළ), where _kola_ = "leaf." Also _Indian pennywort_ (after the rounded leaf shape; not to be confused with _Hydrocotyle_ pennywort, a different but adjacent Apiaceae genus that was for a long time grouped with Centella, which is why Centella was sometimes placed in _Hydrocotyle_).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "gotu kola, n."); the Sinhala etymology is standard. The Centella / Hydrocotyle taxonomic history: the genus _Centella_ was separated from _Hydrocotyle_ in the 20th c.]

*Chinese:* 積雪草 (jīxuěcǎo, "piling-snow herb") — the canonical drug name; also 崩大碗 (bēngdàwǎn) and 老公根 (lǎogōnggēn) in regional Chinese usage; modern transliteration 雷公根 (léigōnggēn) in some sources.

*Sanskrit / Indic — the maṇḍūkaparṇī / brāhmī naming question:* the classical Indian situation is *complex*:
- *मण्डूकपर्णी (_maṇḍūkaparṇī_)*, "frog-leaved" — referring to the small rounded leaf-shape resembling a frog's foot or a frog's back. The *modern dravyaguna consensus identifies maṇḍūkaparṇī with Centella asiatica.*
- *ब्राह्मी (_brāhmī_)*, "of Brahmā / the goddess Brāhmī" — *ambiguously* identified across the classical tradition: in Southern India (Tamil, Kerala, Karnataka traditions), _brāhmī_ historically refers to *Centella asiatica*; in Northern India (Sanskritic / Bengali traditions) and in modern academic dravyaguna, _brāhmī_ refers to *Bacopa monnieri* (Plantaginaceae) — *a completely different plant from a different family*, also a foundational medhya rasāyana drug.
- The Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu treats brāhmī and maṇḍūkaparṇī in adjacent entries, suggesting different drugs but with overlapping uses; the *Caraka and Suśruta texts use the names with the regional ambiguity.*
- *Modern Indian pharmacy* generally uses _brāhmī_ for Bacopa and _maṇḍūkaparṇī_ for Centella, but Western herbal usage and Sri Lankan / South Indian usage may differ.
- Synonyms for maṇḍūkaparṇī: ब्राह्मी (in South Indian usage; the source of the ambiguity), जलवल्ली (_jalavallī_, "water creeper"), दिव्या (_divyā_, "divine"), सरस्वती (_sarasvatī_, "of Sarasvati" — goddess of learning, indicating the medhya / intellect-promoting use).#footnote[The maṇḍūkaparṇī / brāhmī identification ambiguity is one of the famous standing issues in Indian dravyaguna; documented in P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, on both species. The Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu adjacent treatments; the regional usage difference between South and North India. Monier-Williams (s.v. _maṇḍūkaparṇī_, _brāhmī_) records both names.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

積雪草 enters the Bencao tradition through Tang and Song-era materials and is documented in the _Bencao Gangmu_.

*Property:* Cool (涼) to slightly cold. *Flavor:* Bitter (苦), pungent (辛). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎).

*Actions:*
- 清熱利濕 — clears heat and resolves damp (the principal action; for damp-heat skin disorders, jaundice, urinary tract complaints).
- 解毒消腫 — resolves toxicity and reduces swelling.
- 涼血止血 — cools the blood and stops bleeding.
- *Topical:* paste for snake bite, sores, ulcers, contusions — the classical wound-care use, paralleling the modern centellic-triterpene wound-healing pharmacology.

*Cautions:* cool nature — contraindicated in Spleen-yang vacuity-cold; pregnancy.#footnote[積雪草 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Maṇḍūkaparṇī is named in Caraka's _medhya rasāyana_ (intellect-rejuvenative) drugs, a small and distinctive class.#footnote[Maṇḍūkaparṇī in Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1.3 (third pāda of the rasāyana-adhyāya), the _medhya rasāyana_ list — four named drugs: maṇḍūkaparṇī, yaṣṭimadhu (licorice), guḍūcī (Tinospora), śaṅkhapuṣpī (Convolvulus pluricaulis). Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_ entries. Translations: Sharma & Dash (Chowkhamba).]

*Rasa:* Tikta (bitter — dominant), Kaṣāya (astringent), Madhura (sweet — mild).
*Guṇa:* Laghu (light), Snigdha (unctuous — mild, distinctive among bitter drugs).
*Vīrya:* Śīta (cooling).
*Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_; the cooling profile is the basis of the Pitta-pacifying medhya action.]

*Prabhāva:* *Medhya* — the prabhāva-level intellect-promoting and cognition-supporting action; one of the four canonical _medhya rasāyana_ drugs.

*Doṣa:* Pacifies all three doṣas (tridoṣa-śāmaka); particularly Pitta.

*Karma:*
- _Medhya, smṛti-vardhana_ — intellect / memory-promoting.
- _Rasāyana_ — rejuvenative; the daily-tonic register.
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — eye-benefiting.
- _Tvacya, varṇya_ — skin-supportive, complexion-improving — the classical reading consistent with modern dermatological use.
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa_ — wound-healing — the modern centellic-triterpene action vindicates the classical use.
- _Kuṣṭha-ghna_ — anti-skin-disease.
- _Dāha-praśamana, pittahara_ — Pitta-and-burning relieving.
- _Apasmāra-hara, unmāda-hara_ — anti-epileptic and anti-mania use; modest classical mental-health register.
- Used in: chronic skin disease, _kuṣṭha_, ulcers and wounds (internal and external), intellectual support / convalescent cognition, anxiety / mental fatigue, modest dermatology.

=== Viruddha (Modern)

- *(F) Conditional cautions:* heavy doses with sedative herbs.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Hepatotoxicity*: documented case reports of hepatitis with high-dose long-term centella supplements (mostly European case literature from the 2000s onward); rare but real. EFSA and equivalent regulatory bodies have noted; clinical practice limits high-dose extracts to short courses (months, not years).
  - *Pregnancy*: avoid at therapeutic doses (uterine-stimulant theoretical concern; limited safety data).
  - *Drug interactions*: theoretical interaction with sedatives, antidiabetic medications.
  - *Photosensitivity*: rare; documented in case reports.
  - *Apiaceae allergy*: possible cross-reactivity.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Centella is_ not in the medieval Arabic Tibb canon _(species is South Asian / South-East Asian / Madagascan, outside the medieval Arabic geographical reach in active medical use). South-Asian Unani adopts the substance under the Sanskrit-derived names_ brāhmī / maṇḍūkparṇī _from the 18th–19th c. Mughal Unani onward._

*Urdu/Hakeemi:* برہمی (_brāhmī_) — typically refers to Centella in Mughal South-Asian Unani, with the Bacopa-vs-Centella nomenclatural ambiguity (see centella.typ Ayurvedic section) preserved exactly. Also منڈوک پرنی (_maṇḍūkparṇī_).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* — Mughal Unani consensus; converges with the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya.

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain tonic — the principal medhya-rasāyana register preserved in Unani); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-nafsānī* (psychic-spirit tonic); *jālī* (cleansing); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-improving); *muqawwī al-aʿṣāb* (nerve tonic); *muḥaffif li-l-quwwa al-ʿaqliyya* (modest enhancement of intellectual function).

*Istemālāt:* memory weakness; mental fatigue; chronic skin disease (the Avurvedic kuṣṭha-ghna register); chronic ulcers (topical); cosmetic preparations.

*Mawāne':* heavy phlegmatic constitution; pregnancy (precautionary).

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; milk; rose-water.

*Badal:* Bacopa (the brāhmī-confusion species); ashwagandha for nervous indications.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sufūf-i Brāhmī*; *Sharbat-i Brāhmī*; *Khamīrah-i Brāhmī*; *Rowghan-i Brāhmī* (medicated oil) — all paralleling Ayurvedic preparations directly. Modern Indian Unani manufacturer products use brāhmī (variously identifying as Centella or Bacopa).

*Source attestation:* not in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār; Mughal-and-modern South-Asian Unani only — *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, _brāhmī / maṇḍūkparṇī_ entry; CCRUM publications.#footnote[Tibb brāhmī (Centella): post-classical South-Asian Unani adoption from the Ayurvedic tradition; the species-identification ambiguity (Centella vs. Bacopa) is preserved in Unani exactly as it exists in Ayurvedic literature.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *積雪草 single-herb decoction* — 15–30 g; for damp-heat patterns.
- *Topical pastes* — for sores, ulcers, snake bite, burns.
- *In compound formulas* for liver-damp-heat and skin disease.

=== Ayurveda

- *Maṇḍūkaparṇī cūrṇa / svarasa* — powder or fresh-juice; classical preparation as a daily medhya rasāyana, often with honey, ghee, milk.
- *Brāhmī ghṛta* (using brāhmī = Centella in some traditions, Bacopa in others — *check the source*) — medicated ghee; classical preparation for memory, intellect, mental-health support.
- *Saraswatāriṣṭa* — fermented medhya preparation containing brāhmī / maṇḍūkaparṇī.
- *External use*: as paste for wounds and skin disorders.
- *Sri Lankan tradition*: gotu kola sambol (fresh-leaf salad with coconut) is daily food, with the medhya register integrated into daily diet.

=== Western / Modern adoption

No Greco-Roman tradition. *Indian Ocean transmission to Madagascar*: Centella is well-established in Madagascan traditional medicine; *Madagascan use for leprosy wounds* in the 1940s–1960s led to modern Western pharmacological interest. The French pharmacological development of *Madecassol* (asiaticoside-based topical) in the mid-20th century was based on Madagascan and Indo-Asian traditional use; this is the modern Western pharmacology entry point.#footnote[Madagascan use for leprosy wounds and the development of Madecassol: standard pharmacognosy / drug-history literature; the centellic triterpenoid pharmacology and Madecassol clinical use are documented in dermatological literature.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Pentacyclic triterpenoid saponins*: *asiaticoside*, *madecassoside*, *asiatic acid*, *madecassic acid* — the principal active class; the wound-healing and skin-regenerative actives. Standardized extracts (TECA — titrated extract of Centella asiatica; TTFCA — total triterpenic fraction of Centella asiatica) are characterized by these.
- Flavonoids: quercetin, kaempferol derivatives.
- Polyacetylenes.
- Volatile oil.
- Small amounts of caffeic acid derivatives.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy and reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; TECA/TTFCA in dermatological pharmacology literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Wound healing*: well-documented at the preclinical and clinical level; asiaticoside and madecassoside accelerate collagen synthesis, granulation tissue formation, and re-epithelialization. *Madecassol* and equivalent topical products are mainstream dermatological drugs in many countries.
- *Chronic venous insufficiency*: TTFCA has clinical evidence (small RCTs and meta-analyses) for improvement in chronic venous insufficiency; a recognized phlebotonic.
- *Scar reduction / keloid prevention*: modest clinical evidence.
- *Skin aging / cosmetic dermatology*: substantial cosmetic-industry use; clinical evidence modest.
- *Cognitive / anxiolytic*: small clinical evidence for cognitive support and anxiety reduction; modest effect sizes; the classical medhya rasāyana use has modest modern support.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the classical Indian _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ + Chinese 涼血解毒 + Madagascan wound-care tradition + modern centellic-triterpene wound-healing pharmacology converge cleanly on the *wound-and-skin-regenerative* action.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia*: 3–6 g powder/day; juice ~10–20 mL.
- *Standardized extract (TECA/TTFCA)*: 60–120 mg/day for chronic venous insufficiency.
- *Sri Lankan daily-food use*: typical food serving as gotu kola sambol — well-tolerated.
- *Risk groups*: pregnancy (avoid), liver disease (caution with high-dose supplements; hepatotoxicity case literature), concurrent sedatives (theoretical), prolonged high-dose use (limit to courses of months).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

